  "missing_data_treatment": "ONE continuous basin-wide HLS mosaic.  At every vegetated land pixel the newest clear Landsat/Sentinel-2 observation in the rolling window wins; persistent cloud/ocean-adjacent gaps fall back to the most recent valid observation, and snow-covered vegetation renders as dormant winter deep blue.  Cities, bare ground, non-vegetated land and open Great Lakes water stay transparent because leaves do not grow there.",
  "shoreline_mask": "assets/great_lakes_watermask.png \u2014 shared NOAA medium-resolution shoreline water mask (nautical-chart compilation, mean-high-water datum; snapped + polygonized once, full vertex precision, antialiased). Overlay alpha is multiplied by this mask and edge RGB is bled into transparent pixels (anti-fringe for bilinear clients), so every product shares one shoreline.",
  "attribution": "Data: US NOAA. This project is not endorsed by NOAA. See DATA_SOURCES.md for exact products and endpoints.",
  "legend_size": [
    760,
    300
  ],
  "legend_scale_html": "What each color means (one trip around the year, left to right on the key): <b>deep blue</b> = dormant winter; <b>light blue/cyan</b> = spring awakening and bud break; <b>teal-green</b> = leaf emergence; <b>green</b> = growing season; <b>yellow-green/yellow</b> = first color; <b>orange</b> = autumn coloring; <b>red/magenta</b> = peak color into leaf drop; <b>purple</b> = late drop returning to <b>deep-blue</b> dormant (the scale wraps: both ends are winter). Color is the satellite-derived growth stage, not the color of individual trees. Every vegetated land pixel is painted from the newest clear HLS observation in the rolling window; persistent gaps fall back to the most recent valid observation. <b>Transparent = cities, bare ground, open water, or non-vegetated land</b>.",
  "data_nature": "NASA HLS harmonized Landsat/Sentinel-2 observations + derived phenology state",
